  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 249 AM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThe aforementioned heavy rainfall pattern remains in place Wednesday \nnight as a surface low continues to meander across the Gulf leaving \nits attendant stationary boundary draped across the Florida \npeninsula. Model guidance continues to struggle with the exact \nevolution of this feature, and latest runs depict the low remaining \nin the Gulf through the end of the week potentially even into the \nearly weekend. High pressure remains entrenched across the eastern \nseaboard at the surface, and finally begins to break down late week \ninto early this upcoming weekend as a front pushes across the \ncentral United States. This could help lift the Gulf low towards the \nnortheast, although at this time uncertainty remains high regarding \nthe evolution and timing of this motion. PWAT values remain elevated \n(1.7-2.0\") on Thursday and Friday, which could lead to a \ncontinuation of heavy rainfall concerns through even the end of this \nweek.  At this time there is a Marginal Risk of excessive rainfall \nalong the east coast for Thursday and Friday. The risk is heavily \ndependent on the exact placement of the low and attendant stationary \nboundary, so it isn't totally out of the question that the Flash \nFlooding risk increases for the late week time period in subsequent \nupdates. \n\nAs we head into the weekend, most model guidance depicts the Gulf \nlow entering the western Atlantic as high pressure breaks down and a \ncold front sweeps across the central United States. This will allow \nit to propagate towards the NE and usher in a much drier air mass \nfrom the north. PWATs drop between 1-1.3\" by the end of the weekend \ninto early next week which should act to limit widespread shower and \nstorm potential.",
